WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.107 --> 00:00:01.690
SPEAKER: In this
"BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:03.960
we'll be going over all
sorts of useful ways

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.090
to work with data in BigQuery.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.818
A great way to learn is by
doing, so in this episode,

00:00:08.818 --> 00:00:10.860
we'll show you how to get
started with a BigQuery

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:11.360
sandbox.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.984
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.650
BigQuery sandbox
gives you free access

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.050
to try out BigQuery
and use the UI

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.620
without providing a credit card
or using a billing account.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.540
It's a quick way to
get started and try out

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.360
some of the concepts we'll be
talking about in this series.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:36.480
To get started, click on the
link in the description below.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.310
If you're a new
Google Cloud user,

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.710
you'll need to create
an account and a project

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.720
by following the prompts.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.520
Once a project is
created, you'll

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.040
be redirected to the
BigQuery console, where

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.940
you'll see Sandbox in
the top left-hand corner.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.890
If you're returning
Google Cloud user,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.950
create a new project by
selecting the Project dropdown.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.730
Your new project may be created
with a default billing account.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.209
If that's the case, go into the
Billing Account Management page

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:07.990
and select Disable Billing by
following the instructions also

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.400
linked below.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.580
Then use the search
bar within the console

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.080
to head to BigQuery.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.600
Make sure your new project
is selected in the Project

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.490
dropdown, and
there, you will also

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.980
see Sandbox in the
top left-hand corner.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.770
Now that you're in
the BigQuery Sandbox,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.600
you're ready to start querying.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.700
That's all it takes
to get set up.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.280
Because you aren't charged for
using the BigQuery Sandbox,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.200
there are a few caveats.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.260
Mainly, any tables or
views that you create

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.700
will expire after 60 days.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.070
You're also limited to
10 gigabytes of storage,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.540
and one terabyte of
data process each month.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.080
Still a lot of room
to play around with.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.650
These numbers may change, so be
sure to check the documentation

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:51.940
for the latest information.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.770
Other than that,
you can easily start

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.950
working with public data sets,
loading in your own data,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.540
and running some queries.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.840
BigQuery provides publicly
available data sets for anyone

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.600
to analyze covering a
variety of data types,

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.039
from historical weather to taxi
trips taken in New York City.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:11.020
To analyze public
data, just click

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:14.050
on the Add Data button
on the left-hand side

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.060
to see a list of the
publicly available data

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.850
sets that you already
have available.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.590
As a quick example,
let's use the project

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:25.060
sunroof public data set, so
you can see how much sunlight

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.310
hits your roof in a year.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.770
In this sample query,
we're averaging

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.080
the amount of
sunlight per US state,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.750
and ordering the top three
states by the highest sunlight

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.220
potential.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.290
As you can see, the
top three states

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.480
are New Mexico,
Arizona and Nevada.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.630
That's a lot of sunshine.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.510
Don't worry if you're not
sure how we built this query.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.750
In the next episode, we'll
explain this in more detail

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.150
and break down how
to query your data.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.610
Look out for the next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.990
and remember, stay curious.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

